ARQ — third-party notices

ARQ itself is proprietary (see LICENSE). It incorporates and links against
third-party open-source components, each governed by its own licence. The
dependency licensing policy, including the allow-list and the review rule for
weak-copyleft licences, is docs/product/DEPENDENCY-AND-LICENCE-POLICY.md.

## Where the complete list lives

This file deliberately does not restate every dependency. The per-package list
is generated, because a hand-maintained second copy is the one that goes stale:
this file spent the repository's whole implementation history asserting that no
third-party code had been incorporated, while the build depended on hundreds of
packages and the public site listed them correctly.

- `dependency-sbom.json` carries the name, version and licence of every
  installed package. It is written by `pnpm check:dependency-licences`, which
  also enforces the policy allow-list, and is published as a CI artifact.
- The published /legal/open-source page renders that same bill of materials for
  the deployed build, grouped by licence.

`pnpm check:notice-attribution` binds this file to that data. It fails if this
file denies incorporating third-party code while the bill of materials reports
packages, if a licence family present in the build is not listed below, or if a
package under a reviewed-exception licence is not named below. A new licence
family appearing in the build is therefore a review event, not a silent addition.

